# Coefficient embedding sends j(q) over ℚ to j(q) over L

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.coeffEmb_jq` (`b0e612d7-234d-5adf-ab0c-b154cd1e1d4c`), by Claude,
2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `a32be3f5-ed52-5bd9-b716-43aa69032e10`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure. Write $\mathtt{jNum} \in
\mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ for the integral power series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$-type numerator built from
`eisenstein4` and `dedekindEtaUnitInv`, and for a commutative ring $K$ let `jqModC K` be the Laurent
series $q^{-1}\cdot \iota(\mathtt{jNum}\otimes K)$, that is, the product of the Hahn series
$\mathrm{single}\,(-1)\,1$ with the image of $\mathtt{jNum}$ under coefficientwise reduction along
$\mathbb{Z} \to K$, viewed as a Laurent series. Let `jq` be the same construction over $\mathbb{Q}$,
namely $\mathrm{single}\,(-1)\,1$ times the Laurent series attached to $\mathtt{jNumQ} =
\mathtt{jNum}$ mapped along $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$. Let `coeffEmb L` be the ring homomorphism
$\mathbb{Q}((q)) \to L((q))$ obtained by applying the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ to each
coefficient. The theorem asserts that `coeffEmb L` carries `jq` to `jqModC L`: the two routes to the
$q$-expansion of $j$ in $L((q))$, one factoring through $\mathbb{Q}$ and one reducing the integral
series directly, coincide.

This is the compatibility of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ with base change of
coefficients from $\mathbb{Q}$ to an arbitrary field of characteristic zero. It is used throughout
the treatment of the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and of $q$-expansions of forms, for instance in
computing degrees of field extensions generated by $j$ and in transferring $q$-expansion identities
between coefficient fields.

